Records what worked, not just what changed.

Two cost bugs fixed first, both named in the PRD:
  - appends were read-modify-write, quadratic at moment volume. applog.zig
    opens without truncation under an exclusive lock and writes at the end
    offset. oplog, provenance and attribution all had it.
  - a flat tree per capture is ~700KB on a 10k-file repo. Moments store a
    delta against the previous representation with a keyframe every 200, so
    a single-file edit costs under 100 bytes. object.Tree is untouched, so
    every existing Oid and the git bridge still hold.

Losslessness is a test, not a promise: 500 captures across keyframe
boundaries each reconstruct byte-identically against the full-tree hash,
and a mismatch is a hard error rather than partial recovery.

Verdicts are keyed by (tree, tier, command), never by moment, so a tree is
never graded twice across branches, forks or rewinds. Grading COW-clones the
live worktree and reconciles it to the target, keeping build caches warm.

Read-sets are measured from APFS access times inside the clone we own, which
works where injection cannot: DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES is erased by SIP through
